findings.body:
**Check the size estimate after each increment, not only on entry.**

`visit` compares `estimate > REPORT_CAP` only when it enters a node (Line 946). If the last value visited is a large string, Line 947 adds its size and no later `visit` call runs the check. Then `JSON.stringify` (Line 961) allocates the whole serialized body, and Line 962 rejects it only afterwards. A report that is one large string, or ends with one, therefore bypasses the guard. That guard exists to prevent "an unbounded JSON allocation" (Lines 941–942).

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
   const visit = (value: unknown, depth: number) => {
-    if (depth > 64 || estimate > REPORT_CAP) throw new Error('terminal report bound reached');
+    if (depth > 64) throw new Error('terminal report bound reached');
     if (typeof value === 'string') estimate += Buffer.byteLength(value) * 6 + 2;
     else if (value && typeof value === 'object') {
       ...
     } else estimate += 32;
+    if (estimate > REPORT_CAP) throw new Error('terminal report bound reached');
   };
```
</details>

**Do not cache a rejected session-creation promise.**

The code stores `pending` in `terminalSessions` before the async setup finishes (Line 271). The setup calls `validate()` four times, between `mkdir` and `writeFile` (Lines 226–253). If any of those calls throws, or `realpath` or `mkdir` fails, the rejected promise stays in the map for that token. Every later call then fails with the same error. This covers `terminalSession` (Lines 223–224), `removeAddressTerminalCandidate` (Line 523) and `disposeAddressTerminalSession` (Line 564).

A realistic trigger is cancellation. If the owner switches to the terminal phase while the first `recordAddressTerminalEvidence` call is still creating the session, `validateEvidenceAuthority(token, false)` throws "terminal evidence phase refused". The following calls then all fail on the cached error:
- `checkpointAddressTerminalEvidence`
- `removeAddressTerminalCandidate`
- `settleAddressTerminalEvidence`
- `disposeAddressTerminalSession`

These are exactly the cleanup calls cancellation needs. The half-created `cq-address-terminal-*` directory under `commonDir` also stays behind, and `dispose` cannot remove it.

The fix: when setup rejects, remove the map entry, but only if it still holds this promise. Also remove only the paths that this attempt created.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
-    terminalSessions.set(token, pending);
+    const created = pending;
+    terminalSessions.set(token, created);
+    created.catch(() => {
+      if (terminalSessions.get(token) === created) terminalSessions.delete(token);
+    });
   }
   return pending;
```

Inside the IIFE, wrap the code after `mkdir(directory)` in `try`/`catch`. In the `catch`, best-effort remove `config`, `hooks` and `directory` (only the paths this attempt created), then rethrow.
</details>
